speechtext:
-I think I should begin by expressing to the hon. member for Skeena my profound appreciation of the very kind remarks he made concerning myself. I would not of course attribute to him the motive of
4942	HOUSE OF
Supply-Defence Production softening me up intentionally. But, Mr. Chairman, if in my remarks this afternoon I sound somewhat delicate, then I hope the house will understand that I feel very much softened up indeed by the remarks of my hon. friend from Skeena. And if, therefore,
I err on the side of understatement in reviewing some of the things said this afternoon, it will be understood that the technique of the hon. member for Skeena has had its effect.
I am sure that if anyone who has not been following the course of public events had dropped into this chamber this afternoon, after listening to the hon. member for Spadina and the hon. member for Skeena, he would have been able to tell at once, without any further consideration of the subject, that we are fast approaching an election; because those were indeed election speeches. They were the old tub-thumping, campaign, stump speeches. Of their kind they were very good and, I might say, very entertaining. It is the sort of thing that can happen in this house only in the last session before an election.
I say to my hon. friends, in very good part, That it was extremely entertaining to be able to sit over here this afternoon and listen to their speeches. I can well expect that the electors in Spadina and the electors in Skeena will have a most entertaining time .ahead of them during the next few weeks.
I do not propose to review the ground covered so ably and effectively by my colleague the hon. member for Calgary East in his speech this afternoon, following the speech of the hon. member for Spadina. However, I shall touch upon some matters which I think need to be brought into focus and into relationship with the facts. I find this necessary after listening to the speeches of the hon. members for Spadina and Skeena.
First let me put the record straight on this matter of the agenda of the committee. The committee did set up a steering committee, and the steering committee did ponder over all the matters it might take up. At page 7 of the proceedings of the committee a list is set out of the subjects that the steering committee reviewed in that light. Here were the suggested topics:
1.	Construction;
2.	Aircraft production;
3.	Armaments, tanks, ammunition, guns and small arms;
4.	Naval vessels;
5.	Operation Pine Tree;
6.	Training costs-all services;
7.	Recruiting costs;


8.	Soft goods;
9.	Military travelling costs.
It is quite true, as the hon. member for Spadina said, that most of these were suggested by opposition members. I recall suggesting the subject of aircraft production, because the hon. member for York West had very definitely expressed the hope that we would go into that subject. Therefore I put it forward at his request. And the committee had to make some selection, with a view to arranging its work. It had no choice as to the first item, because the house had given it the direction that first it must review the Currie report.
A serious error was made this afternoon by the hon. member for Skeena in his appraisal of the time spent by the committee on the Currie report and matters associated with it. He took issue with the statement of the hon. member for Calgary East that about half the meetings had been spent on the Currie report and related matters. I wish to put him right on that. Of the 27 meetings, 13 were spent on the Currie report and matters referred to therein which included appendix B to that report. It was only when we began the 14th meeting that we launched into the subject of the acquisition of real estate which led eventually to the subject of construction. If my hon. friend has any doubt about that he can look at page 303 of the evidence, where he will see that the 14th meeting on March 10 was the first one at which we launched into the subject of the acquisition of real estate.
